# Line endings, decided once (netctl-style: the reason, not just the rule).
#
# WHAT WENT WRONG WITHOUT THIS. The repository stores CRLF - it was populated from Windows - and the
# Actifsource generator writes LF. So `cleon build` rewrote 452 files with no content change whatsoever
# and left them "modified": `git status` became unreadable after every build, `git checkout <branch>`
# refused to switch, and a drift gate over generated sources could never have told a real change from
# an end-of-line.
#
# `text=auto` makes Git normalise to LF IN THE REPOSITORY and compare normalised, so a working tree that
# holds either kind is clean. It does not force the working tree: a Windows checkout with the usual
# core.autocrlf=true still gets CRLF, which is what tools there expect.
#
# Committing this needed one renormalising commit (`git add --renormalize .`). That is why a single
# commit touches thousands of files and changes not one character of content.
* text=auto

# BATCH FILES ARE CRLF IN THE WORKING TREE, ALWAYS. `cmd.exe` reads a script by byte offset, and a
# `goto` in an LF-only file is the documented way to make it jump somewhere else - cleon.cmd has 20
# labels and gotos in it. The repository stored it LF-only even before the normalisation above, so this
# has been one `core.autocrlf=false` away from breaking on any Windows host, including the runner: what
# saved it so far is that Git for Windows defaults to converting on checkout. `eol=crlf` stops it being
# a default someone else controls.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Binary by declaration rather than by detection. Git guesses well, but a guess that goes wrong here
# corrupts an artefact rather than producing a noisy diff.
*.zip   binary
*.jar   binary
*.png   binary
*.gif   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
